  "body": "Filed by queue triage task 6875 (2026-11-03) from the design record on main, `doc/2026-11-01_design_id2031.md` §2, which names this as \"a prerequisite missing from #3788–#3790's current dependency graph\" — it has no issue of its own, so the gap is invisible to the fleet.\n\n## The gap (per the design record, verified against main 2026-11-03)\n\n- `ChainExt` carries a free-string `kind` and optional group (`pkgs/id/src/chain.rs`, root `id` package), and the signed header commits to a body's hash and size while the body itself is separate.\n- Ingest accepts and verifies a supplied body (`pkgs/id/src/event_store/ingest.rs`), but `store_event` writes `event.bytes` (the signed header) only (`pkgs/id/src/event_store/mod.rs`); range sync sends stored events and fork-proof headers (`pkgs/id/src/event_store/sync.rs`).\n- Consequence: a `didit.*` CBOR body can be validated at ingest but is not yet available for later retrieval or cross-device replication.\n\n## Scope\n\nDesign + implement generic application event-body persistence, retrieval, and range-sync in `pkgs/id` (not didit-specific), so any `kind` with a CBOR body — starting with the four `didit.*` kinds from issue #3788 — round-trips: stored at ingest, retrievable by event id/hash, and carried by range sync with the same integrity checks as headers.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A stored event with a generic body can be retrieved byte-identically after restart.\n- Range sync between two nodes transfers bodies for events the peer lacks, with hash/size verification against the signed header.\n- Existing header-only kinds are unaffected; tests cover body-present and body-absent events.\n\n## Relations\n\n- Blocks the app phase: issue #3790 (Phase 3 of #2031) cannot read tracker state back without this.\n- Does NOT block issue #3788 (Phase 1: library-only schema types + pure evaluator, golden tests only).\n- Parent: issue #2031.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Generic event-body persistence exists for `pkgs/id`: bodies can be stored, retrieved by event id, and range-synced without per-type bespoke code.\n- Retrieval of a missing body returns a typed not-found (not a panic or silent empty).\n- Range-sync is idempotent: re-syncing a range does not duplicate bodies.\n- Covered by tests exercising persistence, retrieval, and a repeated range-sync.",
